  "slug": "reactivity-tcr-45ba",
  "summary": "Single-cell and spatial profiling across cancer types now maps the myeloid, stromal and tumor-intrinsic programs that suppress CD8+ T cells.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is single-cell and spatial dissection of myeloid, stromal and tumor-intrinsic programs that restrict CD8+ T-cell function and anti-PD-1 efficacy. Several studies identify discrete suppressive populations: SRGN+ neutrophils driving CD8+ T-cell exhaustion in glioma, TREM2+ macrophages suppressing CD8+ T cells in cholangiocarcinoma and HCC, M-MDSCs driving EMT via THBS1-SDC4 in HCC, and MGP-high cancer-associated fibroblasts modulating MDSC recruitment in melanoma. Other items address checkpoint regulation directly, including RNF152-mediated K48 ubiquitination of PD-L1 in ccRCC and GANAB-dependent PD-L1 N-glycosylation in bladder cancer. What is new is the linkage of these populations to specific, testable axes, such as CCL5-driven CD8+ infiltration after HK2 inhibition and AhR blockade boosting IFN-γ CD8+ T cells. The items are heterogeneous in scope, and not all share this mechanism.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-29"
}
